<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Chee Lai! (Paul Robeson and Liu Liangmo, Keynote Records)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation, recorded in Chinese and English</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The 400 Million (Joris Ivens)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1939</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Pathé recording used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Battle of China (Frank Capra)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in, played by the Army Air Force Orchestra</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dragon Seed (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Songbook with English translation (Lee Pao-chen, Chongqing)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1939</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Version with rewritten words (5th National People's Congress)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1978</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same melody; used until 1982</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nie Er (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1959</span><span class="sg-chain-h">biopic of the composer</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The National Anthem (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1999</span><span class="sg-chain-h">retells the song's making</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Great Wall (poem by Tian Han)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1934</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its words are two stanzas of the poem</span></li><li><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a><span class="sg-chain-h">opening motif inspired by it</span></li></ul></section>
